  {
    "lesson_id": "002",
    "trigger_word": "kabar",
    "trigger_card_number": 11,
    "title": "LESSON 2: THE MAIN THING FIRST",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Indonesian puts the main thing first and",
          "hangs the description behind it. This is",
          "the reverse of English, and it never",
          "varies.",
          "",
          "   rumah besar   = a big house",
          "   orang baik    = a good person",
          "   anak kecil    = a small child",
          "",
          "Owning works the same way: the owner",
          "comes second.",
          "",
          "   nama saya     = my name",
          "   rumah kami    = our house",
          "",
          "So 'Nama saya Budi' reads, word for word,",
          "'name my Budi', and it means My name is",
          "Budi."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Indonesian needs no word for is or are.",
          "You put the two halves side by side.",
          "",
          "   Saya Budi.        = I am Budi.",
          "   Rumah itu besar.  = That house is big.",
          "",
          "Before a noun, careful writing may add",
          "adalah: Bumi adalah rumah kita. = The",
          "Earth is our home. Never before an",
          "adjective: Rumah itu besar, nothing more.",
          "",
          "ini is this and itu is that. They follow",
          "the noun: rumah ini = this house.",
          "",
          "For there is and there are, Indonesian",
          "does have a word of its own: ada.",
          "",
          "   Ada dua orang. = There are two people."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
