  {
    "lesson_id": "004",
    "trigger_word": "nama",
    "trigger_card_number": 26,
    "title": "LESSON 4: THE PEOPLE WORDS",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 4,
        "content": [
          "Indonesian pronouns never change shape.",
          "What changes is which one you may use.",
          "",
          "   saya    I, to anyone at all",
          "   aku     I, to family and close friends",
          "   Anda    you, formal and written",
          "   kamu    you, to friends and children",
          "   dia     he or she, one word for both",
          "   mereka  they",
          "",
          "saya is never wrong. aku between",
          "strangers is too warm, the way a first",
          "name can be too warm in English.",
          "",
          "Anda is correct but stiff in speech. In a",
          "shop or on the street Indonesians say Pak",
          "(sir) and Bu (madam) instead, and mean it",
          "warmly."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 4,
        "content": [
          "There are two words for we, and the",
          "difference matters at every table.",
          "",
          "   kami  = we, but not you",
          "   kita  = we, and you as well",
          "",
          "'Ayo, kita ke sana!' invites you along.",
          "'Kami ke sana' leaves you behind, and",
          "English cannot make the distinction.",
          "",
          "To say my, your or his, put the pronoun",
          "after the thing owned:",
          "",
          "   nama saya   = my name",
          "   rumah kami  = our house",
          "   anak Anda   = your child",
          "",
          "   Nama saya Budi. = My name is Budi."
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 3,
        "total_pages": 4,
        "content": [
          "Three of the pronouns also come as tails,",
          "glued to the end of the noun.",
          "",
          "   -ku   = my      rumahku  = my house",
          "   -mu   = your    temanmu  = your friend",
          "   -nya  = his, her, its, or 'the'",
          "",
          "   Rumahku di Jakarta.",
          "   = My house is in Jakarta.",
          "   Temanmu orang baik.",
          "   = Your friend is a good person.",
          "",
          "-nya is the busiest ending in the",
          "language. Besides his and her it marks a",
          "thing already spoken of, much as English",
          "uses the:",
          "",
          "   namanya = his name, or just 'the name'"
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 4,
        "total_pages": 4,
        "content": [
          "Two more pronouns complete the set.",
          "",
          "   kalian  you all, to a group",
          "   beliau  he or she, with respect",
          "",
          "kalian is the only plural you: a teacher",
          "says it to the class, you say it to two",
          "friends. beliau is for a teacher, an",
          "elder or an official, never yourself.",
          "",
          "People are counted with orang:",
          "",
          "   seorang teman   = one friend",
          "   dua orang anak  = two children",
          "",
          "se- means one. Lesson 16 brings the",
          "other counting words."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
