  {
    "lesson_id": "008",
    "trigger_word": "lihat",
    "trigger_card_number": 85,
    "title": "LESSON 8: THE ACTIVE PREFIX",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "The card gives you lihat. The sentence",
          "gives you melihat. They are the same",
          "word, and both are on this card.",
          "",
          "   Saya bisa melihat rumahmu dari sini.",
          "   = I can see your house from here.",
          "",
          "The prefix is written meN-. The N is not",
          "a letter you say; it is a slot that takes",
          "its shape from the sound the root begins",
          "with. Six shapes cover the language.",
          "",
          "   me-    before l, m, n, r, w, y",
          "   mem-   before b, f, p",
          "   men-   before c, d, j, t",
          "   meng-  before g, h, k and vowels",
          "   meny-  before s",
          "   menge- before one-syllable roots"
        ]
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "Watch it work on roots you already have.",
          "",
          "lihat  ->  melihat, to see            Press 1",
          "buat   ->  membuat, to make           Press 2",
          "tunggu ->  menunggu, to wait          Press 3",
          "cari   ->  mencari, to look for       Press 4",
          "",
          "Four of the six shapes eat the first",
          "letter of the root. p, t, k and s vanish",
          "into the prefix and do not come back.",
          "",
          "   pakai  -> memakai   (the p is gone)",
          "   tunggu -> menunggu  (the t is gone)",
          "   kirim  -> mengirim  (the k is gone)",
          "",
          "That is why a word in a sentence can look",
          "nothing like the card. Peel the prefix,",
          "put the letter back, and the root is there."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Lihat. Melihat.",
          "2": "Buat. Membuat.",
          "3": "Tunggu. Menunggu.",
          "4": "Cari. Mencari."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 3,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "The bare root is not wrong. It is the",
          "everyday spoken form, and for a good many",
          "verbs it is the only form anyone uses:",
          "",
          "   makan, minum, tidur, pergi, datang,",
          "   duduk, mandi, pulang",
          "",
          "Nobody says memakan about dinner.",
          "",
          "Where the card shows a bare root and the",
          "sentence shows meN-, learn both and use",
          "whichever the sentences around you are",
          "using. The notes on every verb card name",
          "the root, so the family stays visible:",
          "",
          "   kerja -> bekerja, pekerja, pekerjaan",
          "",
          "That family is Tier 4's whole business."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
